I'm not a psu person but I DID write those messages, so maybe I know
what's going on.

In article <5168@eagle.wesleyan.edu> jochterski@eagle.wesleyan.edu writes:
>This is a warning regarding NH3.0 PL6 from psuvm.psu.edu..
>
>I don't know if there is a virus or just some bugs, but my system behaves
>flakey after I use NH.

To the best of my knowledge, bugs (they're fixed in the next release).
How much fun would it be to write a virus that just made the machine
flakey? (Ok, so the joke's in very poor taste).

>Symptom #1
>   Many times my system hangs (requiring cold reboot) if I run a program after
>running nethack.

If by "many times" you mean perhaps one time in sixteen, then the
problem is a known bug: we sometimes 'restore' the wrong interrupt
vector when we unload the overlay manager. I have trouble imagining
this having serious effects beyond a crash, though. If you're REAL
paranoid, just reboot after each game. As I say, next release, we fix
- the fix is already written and being tested. It may comfort you to
know that the overlay manager currently ONLY opens files for read.

>Symptom#2
>   After I ran another program _after_ running Nethack, I got the message:
>
>"A Nymph just stole your .EXE file"
>I didn't notice any changes to my disk, but there may have been some..

There were no changes to your disk. That message is supposed to be
issued, for example, if you shell out and delete your .exe file while
playing a game. It indicates that the overlay manager tried to read an
overlay from the .exe file, and it wasn't there any more. (In this
case, of course, the programme was no longer running and the file had
been closed.... No wonder it wasn't found). Whatever programme you ran
later must have coincidentally been trying to use the same interrupt
vector as we do....

>I searched the NETHACK.EXE file for this text, and found it, as well as some
>other text about a dog stealing memory or something like that.
>
>I hope this was a joke. In any case an explanation is in order.

Of course it's not a joke. It's a documented error message. Evidently
you neither read the distribution documentation nor the discussion of
those messages I posted here last week.

>I scanned nethack.exe for any known viruses, but there was none.

Of course you didn't. If you're so paranoid about viruses you really
SHOULDN'T be running software you didn't compile with a compiler whose
object code you have read personally. There are viruses that *aren't*
known yet, now, aren't there? But *I* didn't put one in that code, and
I *DID* put in those messages, and I *DID* tell y'all what they mean,
and you *DO* have access to the source for the whole programme! Your
public-mindedness is commendable BUT your delivery is a little
tabloid-oid....

				~ * ~

Please understand that this is only half a response: to be truly
CERTAIN we need assurances from psu as well. But since you are
experiencing expected behaviour in the face of a known bug, I don't
think you need to worry too much. I hope so, anyway.
